OAI_PAPER({"slug": "The-periodic-spin-one-Haldane-gap-September-24-2026", "title": "The periodic spin-one Haldane gap", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-periodic-spin-one-Haldane-gap-September-24-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "268", "family_title": "The spin-one Haldane gap", "abstract_html": "We prove a uniform positive spectral gap for the pure antiferromagnetic spin-one Heisenberg chain on even periodic rings, establishing the positive even-periodic formulation of the spin-one Haldane conjecture.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "the periodic spin-one Haldane gap", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S^x,S^y,S^z\\in M_3(\\mathbb C)\\) be the self-adjoint irreducible spin-one matrices, normalized by\n  \\[\n  [S^\\alpha,S^\\beta]\n  =i\\sum_\\eta\\varepsilon_{\\alpha\\beta\\eta}S^\\eta,\n  \\qquad\n  \\sum_\\alpha(S^\\alpha)^2=2I_3.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For every even integer \\(L\\ge4\\), let\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal H_L=(\\mathbb C^3)^{\\otimes L},\n  \\qquad\n  H_L=\\sum_{j=0}^{L-1}\\sum_{\\alpha=x,y,z}\n  S_j^\\alpha S_{j+1}^\\alpha,\n  \\qquad S_L^\\alpha=S_0^\\alpha.\n  \\]\n  The coupling constant is one.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E_0(L)\\) be the least energy, let \\(E_1(L)\\) be the next distinct energy, and define\n  \\[\n  \\gamma_L=E_1(L)-E_0(L),\n  \\qquad\n  \\Delta_1=\\liminf_{\\substack{L\\to\\infty\\\\L\\text{ even}}}\\gamma_L.\n  \\]\n  These definitions concern the full spectrum, including the multiplicity of the ground energy. The Hamiltonian is not scalar: the all-zero and all-\\(+1\\) product vectors in the \\(S^z\\) basis have expectations \\(0\\) and \\(L\\), respectively.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The ground state is unique for every even \\(L\\ge60\\), and\n\\[\n\\gamma_L&gt;\\frac4{105}\\log\\frac{80}{79}\n\\qquad\n(L\\ge60,\\ L\\text{ even}),\n\\]\nwhile\n\\[\n\\gamma_L&gt;\\frac{\\log20}{784}\n\\qquad\n(L\\ge2304,\\ L\\text{ even}).\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\Delta_1\\ge\\frac{\\log20}{784}&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p>The spectral conclusions also hold for any simultaneously similar spin-one realization satisfying the same commutator and Casimir normalizations.</p><p>In particular,\n\\[\n\\inf_{\\substack{L\\ge4\\\\L\\text{ even}}}\\gamma_L&gt;0.\n\\]\nFor the finitely many lengths below \\(60\\), this last observation supplies neither a numerical lower bound nor a simplicity assertion.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 2 identifies the physical partition functions with moments of a self-adjoint spatial transfer operator. Thus they satisfy the partition-function assumptions of Proposition 3. The Hamiltonians are self-adjoint and non-scalar.</p><p>Proposition 7 gives\n\\[\nS(60,105/4)&gt;\\frac78,\n\\qquad\nT(120,105/4)&gt;\\frac78.\n\\]\nApply Proposition 3 with\n\\[\n(n_0,b_0,u_0,C)\n=\n\\left(60,\\frac{105}{4},\\frac18,\\frac{79}{10}\\right).\n\\]\nThe scalar hypotheses hold:\n\\[\nG(1/8)=\\frac{913952}{117649}&lt;\\frac{79}{10},\n\\]\n\\[\nr=Cu_0=\\frac{79}{80}&lt;1,\n\\qquad\n\\frac3{C(1-3u_0)}=\\frac{48}{79}&lt;1.\n\\]\nTherefore the ground state is unique and\n\\[\n\\gamma_L&gt;\\frac4{105}\\log\\frac{80}{79}\n\\]\nfor every even \\(L\\ge60\\).</p><p>Proposition 8 gives both required purity defects at most \\(1/100\\) at the pair based at\n\\[\n(n_0,b_0)=(2304,784).\n\\]\nUse Proposition 3 with \\(u_0=1/100\\) and \\(C=5\\). For \\(0&lt;u\\le1/100\\),\n\\[\n1-(1-u)^3\\le3u\n\\]\nand hence\n\\[\nG(u)\\le\\frac9{2(1-3u)^2}\n\\le\\frac{45000}{9409}&lt;5.\n\\]\nAlso,\n\\[\nr=Cu_0=\\frac1{20},\n\\qquad\n\\frac3{C(1-3u_0)}=\\frac{60}{97}&lt;1.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n\\gamma_L&gt;\\frac{\\log20}{784}\n\\]\nfor every even \\(L\\ge2304\\). Taking the limit inferior proves\n\\[\n\\Delta_1\\ge\\frac{\\log20}{784}.\n\\]</p><p>Proposition 1 transfers these spectral conclusions to simultaneously similar spin-one realizations by tensor-power similarity.</p><p>Finally, each of the finitely many remaining finite-dimensional non-scalar Hamiltonians has a positive gap above its full ground sector. Combining those finitely many gaps with the uniform estimate gives\n\\[\n\\inf_{\\substack{L\\ge4\\\\L\\text{ even}}}\\gamma_L&gt;0,\n\\]\nwithout an additional numerical bound or simplicity assertion at the small lengths.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "periodic inputs for boundary estimates", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  a=\\frac{700741}{500000},\n  \\qquad\n  b_0=\\frac{49}{4},\n  \\qquad\n  b_1=\\frac{49}{2}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  P_x=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1),\\quad\n  P_y=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,-1),\\quad\n  P_z=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,-1,1),\n  \\]\n  and \\(D_2=\\{I,P_x,P_y,P_z\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(b&gt;0\\), let \\(\\Omega_b\\) consist of finite ordered histories\n  \\[\n  ((t_1,\\alpha_1),\\ldots,(t_k,\\alpha_k)),\n  \\qquad\n  0&lt;t_1&lt;\\cdots&lt;t_k&lt;b,\n  \\qquad\n  \\alpha_j\\in\\{x,y,z\\},\n  \\]\n  including the empty history. Give each labeled simplex Lebesgue measure and the empty history mass one.</p><p class=\"li\">In Cartesian coordinates, put\n  \\[\n  (S^\\alpha)_{ij}=-i\\varepsilon_{\\alpha ij},\n  \\qquad G_\\alpha=iS^\\alpha.\n  \\]\n  On \\(L^2(\\Omega_b,\\mu;\\mathbb C)\\), let \\(K_b\\) have kernel equal to the trace of the chronologically merged product of the \\(G_\\alpha\\)'s from its two histories, with value zero on nonempty time collisions. Set\n  \\[\n  X_b=e^{-ab}K_b.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The signed label action of proper signed permutation matrices gives the commuting unitary representation described in Proposition 2 below.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(J\\) denote the eigenvalue list of \\(X_{b_0}\\) on the \\(D_2\\)-invariant subspace. Let \\(N\\) denote its eigenvalue list on any one nontrivial \\(D_2\\)-character subspace.</p><p class=\"li\">For the untwisted periodic Hamiltonian, define\n  \\[\n  Z_n(b)=\\operatorname{Tr}e^{-b(H_n+anI)},\n  \\qquad\n  S(n,b)=\\frac{Z_{2n}(b)}{Z_n(b)^2}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\ell=\\frac{999}{1000},\\qquad\n  U=\\frac{100139}{100000},\\qquad\n  V=\\frac{872}{1000},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  m_J=1.0657205,\\qquad m_N=0.2783155.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At \\(b=b_0\\), the full eigenvalue list, counted with multiplicity, is the list \\(J\\) together with three identical lists \\(N\\). Every eigenvalue in \\(J\\) has modulus less than \\(U\\), and\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\lambda\\in J}\\lambda^{12}\\le m_J.\n\\]\nEvery eigenvalue in each \\(N\\) list has modulus less than \\(V\\), and\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\lambda\\in N}\\lambda^{12}\\le m_N.\n\\]\nThere is an eigenvalue in \\(J\\) whose modulus exceeds \\(\\ell\\). Moreover,\n\\[\n1-S(72,b_1)\\le\\frac{151249}{2500000}.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(n=72\\) and \\(n=120\\),\n\\[\nE_0(n)+na&lt;0.\n\\]\nHence a physical ground Boltzmann factor in \\(Z_n(b)\\) exceeds one for every \\(b&gt;0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 2 gives the decomposition into the \\(D_2\\)-invariant list \\(J\\) and three identical nontrivial-character lists \\(N\\).</p><p>The intermediate conclusions of Proposition 8 give the \\(J\\)- and \\(N\\)-modulus bounds, their twelfth-moment bounds, and a \\(J\\)-eigenvalue with modulus greater than \\(\\ell\\). The first row of its rounded-update table gives\n\\[\n1-S(72,49/2)\\le\\frac{151249}{2500000}.\n\\]</p><p>Proposition 5 gives\n\\[\nE_0(72)+72a&lt;0,\n\\qquad\nE_0(120)+120a&lt;0.\n\\]\nTherefore the corresponding physical ground Boltzmann factors exceed one for every positive inverse temperature.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "an infinite-volume consequence", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the self-adjoint periodic Hamiltonians of Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Place the ring of even length \\(L\\) on\n  \\[\n  \\{-L/2,\\ldots,L/2-1\\},\n  \\]\n  with its closing bond at the endpoints.</p><p class=\"li\">A local operator on the chain \\(\\mathbb Z\\) acts nontrivially on only finitely many sites.</p><p class=\"li\">A state \\(\\omega\\) on local operators is a linear functional satisfying\n  \\[\n  \\omega(I)=1,\\qquad \\omega(A^*A)\\ge0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define the local commutator\n  \\[\n  [H,A]=\\sum_{j\\in\\mathbb Z}[S_j\\cdot S_{j+1},A].\n  \\]\n  Only finitely many summands are nonzero.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The normalized ground states of the even periodic rings have a subsequence, as \\(L\\to\\infty\\), converging on every local operator to a state \\(\\omega\\). Every such limit satisfies\n\\[\n\\omega(A^*[H,A])\n\\ge\n\\frac{\\log20}{784}\n\\left(\\omega(A^*A)-|\\omega(A)|^2\\right)\n\\]\nfor every local operator \\(A\\).</p><p>In particular, \\(\\omega\\) is a ground state, and for \\(\\omega(A)=0\\) the displayed inequality gives the locally unique gapped property with gap at least \\((\\log20)/784. The assertion does not identify limits of different subsequences or establish uniqueness among all infinite-volume ground states.</p>", "proof": "<p>Compactness of the density matrices on every finite interval and a diagonal subsequence give convergence on all local operators. The compatible limits define a positive normalized functional \\(\\omega\\).</p><p>For even \\(L\\ge2304\\), Theorem 1 gives a unique normalized ground-state vector \\(\\psi_L\\) and a gap greater than\n\\[\n\\delta=\\frac{\\log20}{784}.\n\\]\nWrite\n\\[\n\\omega_L(A)=\\langle\\psi_L,A\\psi_L\\rangle.\n\\]\nThe spectral inequality on the orthogonal complement of \\(\\psi_L\\) gives\n\\[\n\\omega_L(A^*[H_L,A])\n\\ge\n\\delta\\left(\\omega_L(A^*A)-|\\omega_L(A)|^2\\right).\n\\]</p><p>For fixed local \\(A\\) and sufficiently large \\(L\\), the closing bond commutes with \\(A\\), and\n\\[\n[H_L,A]=[H,A].\n\\]\nPassing to the local limit proves\n\\[\n\\omega(A^*[H,A])\n\\ge\n\\frac{\\log20}{784}\n\\left(\\omega(A^*A)-|\\omega(A)|^2\\right).\n\\]</p><p>In particular, no local perturbation lowers the energy. When \\(\\omega(A)=0\\), the inequality is the locally unique gapped property with the stated lower bound. The argument makes no assertion identifying different subsequential limits or proving uniqueness among all infinite-volume ground states.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Spin coordinates, twisted Hamiltonians, and uniform energy bounds.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Spin coordinates, twisted Hamiltonians, and uniform energy bounds.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S^x,S^y,S^z\\in M_3(\\mathbb C)\\) satisfy\n  \\[\n  [S^\\alpha,S^\\beta]\n  =i\\sum_\\gamma\\varepsilon_{\\alpha\\beta\\gamma}S^\\gamma,\n  \\qquad\n  \\sum_\\alpha(S^\\alpha)^2=2I.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For the self-adjoint realization, write\n  \\[\n  h=\\sum_\\alpha S^\\alpha\\otimes S^\\alpha.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(n\\ge4\\) and \\(g\\in SO(3)\\), define\n  \\[\n  H_n(g)=\\sum_{j=0}^{n-2}h_{j,j+1}+h_{n-1,0}(g),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  h_{n-1,0}(g)\n  =\\sum_\\alpha S_{n-1}^\\alpha(gS^\\alpha g^{-1})_0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  a=\\frac{700741}{500000},\n  \\qquad\n  Z_n(b,g)=\\operatorname{Tr}e^{-b(H_n(g)+anI)},\n  \\qquad\n  Z_n(b)=Z_n(b,I),\n  \\qquad b&gt;0.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The matrices \\(S^\\alpha\\) are simultaneously similar to the standard self-adjoint spin-one matrices; if they are already self-adjoint, the similarity can be chosen unitary. Tensor-power similarity preserves the full spectra of the corresponding periodic Hamiltonians.</p><p>In Cartesian coordinates,\n\\[\n(S^\\alpha)_{ij}=-i\\varepsilon_{\\alpha ij},\n\\qquad\nG_\\alpha=iS^\\alpha,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n-h=\\sum_\\alpha G_\\alpha\\otimes G_\\alpha,\n\\qquad\nh=F-|\\Omega\\rangle\\langle\\Omega|,\n\\qquad\n\\Omega=\\sum_{i=1}^3|ii\\rangle.\n\\]\nHere \\(F(u\\otimes v)=v\\otimes u\\). Each \\(G_\\alpha\\) is real with operator norm one, and \\(h\\) has eigenvalues \\(-2,-1,1\\).</p><p>For every \\(n\\ge4\\) and \\(g\\in SO(3)\\),\n\\[\n-\\frac{3n}{2}I\\le H_n(g)\\le nI.\n\\]</p><p>The partition functions above are strictly positive. For \\(P=P_x\\) and for the cyclic rotation \\(C:e_x\\mapsto e_y\\mapsto e_z\\mapsto e_x\\), global rotations identify the twists with \\(e^{-i\\theta S^z}\\), where respectively \\(\\theta=\\pi\\) and \\(\\theta=2\\pi/3\\). In the weight basis, the diagonal entry at a word \\(x\\) is\n\\[\n\\sum_jx_{j-1}x_j,\n\\]\nand each allowed opposite-unit nearest-neighbor step has coefficient one, except that a seam step from column \\(y\\) to row \\(x\\) has coefficient\n\\[\ne^{i\\theta(y_0-x_0)}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 1.1 supplies the representation and spectral-invariance assertions.</p><p>In the weight basis \\(|s\\rangle\\), \\(s\\in\\{-1,0,1\\}\\),\n\\[\nS^z|s\\rangle=s|s\\rangle,\n\\]\nand the nonzero ladder entries are \\(\\sqrt2\\). In Cartesian coordinates, contraction of the epsilon tensors gives\n\\[\n-h=\\sum_\\alpha G_\\alpha\\otimes G_\\alpha,\n\\qquad\nh=F-|\\Omega\\rangle\\langle\\Omega|.\n\\]\nConsequently the eigenvalues of \\(h\\) are \\(-2,-1,1\\), and \\(h\\le I\\). Lemma 1.2 gives the uniform bounds for all twists.</p><p>The twisted Hamiltonians are self-adjoint, so their exponential traces are positive. The three nonidentity elements of \\(D_2\\) have equal partition functions, by a global cyclic rotation of all sites. The rotation \\(C\\) has angle \\(2\\pi/3\\) about the diagonal axis. After global rotation, \\(P\\) and \\(C\\) therefore have the stated weight-basis forms.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\ngS^\\pm g^{-1}=e^{\\mp i\\theta}S^\\pm\n\\]\ngives the seam coefficient \\(e^{i\\theta(y_0-x_0)}\\); the diagonal and ordinary hopping entries follow from the spin-one ladder entries.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "choice of representation", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(S^x,S^y,S^z\\in M_3(\\mathbb C)\\) satisfy the commutator and Casimir identities in Proposition 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>They are simultaneously similar to the standard self-adjoint spin-one matrices. If the original matrices are self-adjoint, the similarity can be chosen unitary. The corresponding tensor-power similarity preserves the complete spectrum of every periodic Hamiltonian \\(H_n\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(S^\\pm=S^x\\pm iS^y\\). The assumptions give\n\\[\n[S^z,S^\\pm]=\\pm S^\\pm,\n\\]\n\\[\nS^-S^+=2I-(S^z)^2-S^z,\n\\qquad\nS^+S^-=2I-(S^z)^2+S^z.\n\\]</p><p>Choose an \\(S^z\\)-eigenvector \\(v\\) whose eigenvalue \\(m\\) has maximal real part. Then \\(S^+v=0\\), since otherwise it has eigenvalue \\(m+1\\). Thus \\(m=1\\) or \\(m=-2\\).</p><p>The latter case would permit indefinite nonzero lowering: a last nonzero lowered vector would have weight \\(-2-k\\), whereas \\(S^+S^-w=0\\) forces its weight to be \\(-1\\) or \\(2\\). This contradicts finite dimension. Hence \\(m=1\\).</p><p>The vectors\n\\[\nv,\\qquad \\frac{S^-v}{\\sqrt2},\\qquad\\frac{(S^-)^2v}{2}\n\\]\nare nonzero by the displayed identities and have distinct weights \\(1,0,-1\\). They form a basis. There is no further lowered vector because the basis has no weight \\(-2\\). The identities give raising and lowering coefficients \\(\\sqrt2\\), so this is the standard spin-one representation.</p><p>If the original matrices are self-adjoint and \\(\\|v\\|=1\\), these three vectors are orthonormal. Finally, conjugating each tensor factor conjugates every term of \\(H_n\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "energy bounds", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(H_n(g)\\) be the self-adjoint twisted Hamiltonian defined in Proposition 1, with \\(n\\ge4\\) and \\(g\\in SO(3)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n-\\frac{3n}{2}I\\le H_n(g)\\le nI.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The upper bound follows from \\(h\\le I\\) and unitary conjugation of the seam.</p><p>For the lower bound, consider\n\\[\nT=-h_{12}-h_{23}\n\\]\non three open sites in Cartesian coordinates. From\n\\[\nh=F-|\\Omega\\rangle\\langle\\Omega|,\n\\]\nan unequal pair swaps with coefficient \\(-1\\), and an equal pair changes to either of the other equal pairs with coefficient \\(1\\). There are no diagonal terms.</p><p>Assign a positive weight \\(q\\) to each three-letter word. The following table lists every equality pattern; \\(a,b,c\\) denote distinct letters:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n\\text{word type}&amp;q&amp;\\text{absolute weighted row sum}\\\\ \\hline\naaa&amp;4&amp;3+3+3+3=12\\\\\naab\\text{ or }abb&amp;3&amp;4+3+2=9\\\\\naba&amp;2&amp;3+3=6\\\\\nabc&amp;2&amp;2+2=4\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nEvery row sum is at most \\(3q\\). If \\(D\\) is the diagonal matrix of these weights, then \\(D^{-1}TD\\) has absolute row sums at most three. Its spectral radius is therefore at most three. Since \\(T\\) is self-adjoint and similar to this matrix,\n\\[\nh_{12}+h_{23}\\ge-3I.\n\\]</p><p>An open triad containing the seam is unitarily equivalent to an untwisted triad: delete the seam bond and rotate all sites in one of the two resulting components. The internal interaction of that component is rotation invariant. Thus the same lower bound holds for every consecutive pair of bonds in \\(H_n(g)\\). Summing the \\(n\\) triad inequalities counts each bond twice and proves the lower bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "spatial transfer and rotation sectors", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(a\\), \\(H_n(g)\\), and \\(Z_n(b,g)\\) from Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal G\\subset SO(3)\\) be the group of proper signed permutation matrices. For \\(g\\in\\mathcal G\\), write\n  \\[\n  ge_\\alpha=\\epsilon_g(\\alpha)e_{\\sigma_g(\\alpha)},\n  \\qquad \\epsilon_g(\\alpha)\\in\\{-1,1\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(D_2\\), \\(P=P_x\\), and \\(C\\) be the rotations defined above.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For each \\(b&gt;0\\), there are a Hilbert space \\(\\mathcal K_b\\), a compact self-adjoint Hilbert–Schmidt operator \\(X_b\\), and a unitary representation \\(g\\mapsto U_g\\) of \\(\\mathcal G\\), commuting with \\(X_b\\), such that\n\\[\nZ_n(b,g)=\\operatorname{Tr}(X_b^nU_g)\n\\qquad(n\\ge4,\\ g\\in\\mathcal G).\n\\]\nBoth \\(X_b\\) and the representation commute with complex conjugation. Every \\(X_b^k\\), \\(k\\ge2\\), is trace class. If \\(\\lambda_i(b)\\) are the nonzero eigenvalues, counted with multiplicity, then\n\\[\n\\sum_i|\\lambda_i(b)|^k&lt;\\infty\n\\qquad(k\\ge2),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nZ_n(b)=\\sum_i\\lambda_i(b)^n\n\\qquad(n\\ge4).\n\\]</p><p>There are real eigenvalue lists with absolutely convergent integer moments \\(I_k,O_k,N_k\\), \\(k\\ge2\\), such that\n\\[\nZ_n(b)=I_n+2O_n+3N_n,\n\\]\n\\[\nZ_n(b,P)=I_n+2O_n-N_n,\n\\]\n\\[\nZ_n(b,C)=I_n-O_n\n\\qquad(n\\ge4).\n\\]\nThe \\(I\\) list is the restriction to the joint invariant subspace of \\(D_2\\) and \\(C\\). The \\(O\\) list occurs twice and the \\(N\\) list three times in the full list. All three moments are nonnegative at even orders.</p><p>If \\(J_k\\) denotes the moment on the \\(D_2\\)-invariant subspace, then\n\\[\nJ_k=I_k+2O_k.\n\\]\nFor even \\(n\\ge4\\),\n\\[\np_i=\\frac{|\\lambda_i(b)|^n}{Z_n(b)}\n\\]\nare probabilities, and\n\\[\n\\sum_i p_i^2=\\frac{Z_{2n}(b)}{Z_n(b)^2}.\n\\]</p><p>The sector moments are recovered from twisted traces by\n\\[\nJ_n=\\frac{Z_n+3Z_n(P)}4,\\qquad\nN_n=\\frac{Z_n-Z_n(P)}4,\n\\]\n\\[\nI_n=\\frac{Z_n+3Z_n(P)+8Z_n(C)}{12},\n\\qquad\nO_n=\\frac{Z_n+3Z_n(P)-4Z_n(C)}{12},\n\\]\nat a common inverse temperature.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 2.1 and 2.2 give the spatial transfer representation and its operator properties. The ordinary trace formula for a compact self-adjoint operator gives\n\\[\nZ_n(b)=\\sum_i\\lambda_i(b)^n.\n\\]\nFor even \\(n\\), these summands are nonnegative, so normalization gives the stated spatial probabilities and their purity.</p><p>Lemma 2.3 gives the sector decomposition. Solving its three trace identities gives the displayed formulas for \\(J_n,N_n,I_n,O_n\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**History-kernel construction and rotation action, from the proof of original Proposition 3.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**History-kernel construction and rotation action, from the proof of original Proposition 3.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Fix \\(b&gt;0\\). Let \\(\\Omega_b\\) be the history space in Theorem 2, with measure \\(\\mu\\), and let\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_b=L^2(\\Omega_b,\\mu;\\mathbb C).\n\\]\nFor two histories \\(x,y\\), let \\(k_b(x,y)\\) be the trace of the product of their \\(G_\\alpha\\)'s merged in increasing time, from left to right. Set the kernel to zero on nonempty time collisions; for two empty histories its value is three. Let \\(K_b\\) be its integral operator and \\(X_b=e^{-ab}K_b\\).</p><p>For \\(g\\in\\mathcal G\\), let \\(\\sigma_g\\) act on history labels, and put\n\\[\ns_g(x)=\\prod_{\\alpha\\text{ in }x}\\epsilon_g(\\alpha),\n\\qquad\n(U_gf)(x)=s_g(\\sigma_g^{-1}x)f(\\sigma_g^{-1}x).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The operator \\(X_b\\) is real, self-adjoint, and Hilbert–Schmidt. The operators \\(U_g\\) form a real unitary representation commuting with \\(X_b\\). Moreover,\n\\[\n(K_bU_g)(x,y)=k_b(x,\\sigma_gy)s_g(y).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The total measure is\n\\[\n\\mu(\\Omega_b)=\\sum_{k=0}^\\infty\\frac{3^kb^k}{k!}=e^{3b}.\n\\]\nThe kernel is real and has absolute value at most three, since \\(\\|G_\\alpha\\|=1\\). Exchanging \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) leaves their merged chronological list unchanged. Hence\n\\[\nk_b(x,y)=k_b(y,x);\n\\]\nno reversal of the matrix product occurs. Thus \\(K_b\\) is self-adjoint and Hilbert–Schmidt, with\n\\[\n\\|K_b\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2\\le9e^{6b}.\n\\]\nCompact self-adjoint spectral theory gives real, square-summable eigenvalues. Since they are bounded, all absolute integer moments of order at least two are summable.</p><p>Rotation covariance is\n\\[\ngG_\\alpha g^{-1}\n=\\epsilon_g(\\alpha)G_{\\sigma_g(\\alpha)}.\n\\]\nThe label permutation preserves times and measure. The identity\n\\[\ns_{gh}(x)=s_g(\\sigma_hx)s_h(x)\n\\]\nproves that the \\(U_g\\)'s form a representation. They are real unitaries.</p><p>Simultaneously rotating every matrix inside a site trace gives\n\\[\nk_b(\\sigma_gx,\\sigma_gy)\n=s_g(x)s_g(y)k_b(x,y).\n\\]\nIt follows that \\(U_g\\) commutes with \\(K_b\\) and \\(X_b\\). Changing variables in the integral operator gives\n\\[\n(K_bU_g)(x,y)=k_b(x,\\sigma_gy)s_g(y).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Cycle trace and physical partition-function identity, from original Proposition 3.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Cycle trace and physical partition-function identity, from original Proposition 3.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(K_b,X_b,U_g,\\Omega_b,\\mu\\) from Lemma 2.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(n\\ge2\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}(K_b^nU_g)\n=\n\\int_{\\Omega_b^n}\n\\left(\\prod_{j=0}^{n-2}k_b(x_j,x_{j+1})\\right)\nk_b(x_{n-1},\\sigma_gx_0)s_g(x_0)\\,d\\mu^n.\n\\]\nFor \\(n\\ge4\\), this equals\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}e^{-bH_n(g)}.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\nZ_n(b,g)=\\operatorname{Tr}(X_b^nU_g).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(n\\ge2\\), both \\(K_b^{n-1}\\) and \\(K_bU_g\\) are Hilbert–Schmidt. The kernel of \\(K_b^{n-1}\\) is the iterated kernel obtained by integration; it is bounded because \\(k_b\\) is bounded and \\(\\mu\\) is finite.</p><p>The Hilbert–Schmidt product trace identity\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}(AB)\n=\\int a(x,y)b(y,x)\\,d\\mu(x)d\\mu(y)\n\\]\nand the kernel formula in Lemma 2.1 give the displayed cycle integral. The product trace identity follows from the Hilbert–Schmidt inner product formula and Cauchy–Schwarz. All iterated kernel integrals here are absolutely integrable. Thus no diagonal-kernel formula for \\(K_b\\) itself is required.</p><p>Expand \\(e^{-bH_n(g)}\\) in its ordered-time series. Since\n\\[\n-h=\\sum_\\alpha G_\\alpha\\otimes G_\\alpha,\n\\]\neach event chooses a bond and one of three labels, inserting the corresponding two \\(G\\) factors. At the seam the site-\\(0\\) factor is rotated. At total degree \\(k\\), there are \\((3n)^k\\) choices, and each tensor operator has norm at most one. The sum of absolute traced contributions is bounded by\n\\[\n3^n\\sum_{k=0}^\\infty\\frac{(3nb)^k}{k!}\n=3^ne^{3nb}.\n\\]\nThe terms and integrals may therefore be regrouped by bond. Interleavings of bond histories partition the global time simplex up to null collisions. At each site, the tensor trace is the kernel on its two incident histories, in their original chronological order.</p><p>For the seam, call its history \\(e_0\\) and enumerate the remaining bond histories \\(e_1,\\ldots,e_{n-1}\\). The physical integrand is\n\\[\ns_g(e_0)k_b(\\sigma_ge_0,e_1)\nk_b(e_1,e_2)\\cdots k_b(e_{n-1},e_0).\n\\]\nIn the cycle integral set\n\\[\nx_0=e_0,\\quad x_1=e_{n-1},\\quad\\ldots,\\quad x_{n-1}=e_1\n\\]\nand use kernel symmetry. This gives exactly the physical integrand, with \\(g\\) on site \\(0\\). The spatial enumeration has been reversed; the chronological products have not.</p><p>Thus\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}e^{-bH_n(g)}\n=\\operatorname{Tr}(K_b^nU_g).\n\\]\nMultiplication by \\(e^{-abn}\\) proves the shifted identity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "sector moments", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Fix \\(b&gt;0\\) and the commuting operators of Lemma 2.1. Let\n\\[\n\\omega=e^{2\\pi i/3}.\n\\]\nDecompose \\(\\mathcal K_b\\) into the four character spaces of \\(D_2\\). Within the trivial-character space, decompose further into the \\(1,\\omega,\\overline\\omega\\) eigenspaces of \\(U_C\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The restrictions of \\(X_b\\) to the three nontrivial \\(D_2\\)-character spaces have identical eigenvalue lists, denoted \\(N\\). The restrictions to the \\(\\omega\\) and \\(\\overline\\omega\\) spaces have identical eigenvalue lists, denoted \\(O\\). Denote the list on the \\(1\\)-space by \\(I\\).</p><p>Their moments satisfy all sector identities in Proposition 2 and converge absolutely for every integer order at least two.</p>", "proof": "<p>The four characters \\(\\chi\\) of \\(D_2\\) define orthogonal projections\n\\[\n\\Pi_\\chi=\\frac14\\sum_{g\\in D_2}\\chi(g)U_g.\n\\]\nThey commute with \\(X_b\\) and sum to the identity. Conjugation by \\(U_C\\) permutes the three nontrivial character spaces cyclically. The restrictions of \\(X_b\\) to them are therefore unitarily equivalent; write their common moment as \\(N_k\\).</p><p>In the trivial-character space, the three eigenspaces of \\(U_C\\) reduce \\(X_b\\). Complex conjugation exchanges the \\(\\omega\\) and \\(\\overline\\omega\\) spaces and preserves every real eigenvalue of \\(X_b\\), with multiplicity. Their common moment is \\(O_k\\); the moment on the \\(1\\)-space is \\(I_k\\). Absolute convergence follows from the Hilbert–Schmidt eigenvalue bounds.</p><p>The orthogonal decomposition gives\n\\[\nZ_n=I_n+2O_n+3N_n.\n\\]\nOn the three nontrivial character spaces, a fixed \\(U_P\\) has signs \\(+1,-1,-1\\), while it is the identity on the trivial-character space. Hence\n\\[\nZ_n(P)=I_n+2O_n-N_n.\n\\]\nThe operator \\(U_C\\) permutes the nontrivial spaces, so they contribute zero to \\(\\operatorname{Tr}(X_b^nU_C)\\). On the remaining spaces its coefficients are \\(1,\\omega,\\overline\\omega\\), whose last two sum to \\(-1\\). Therefore\n\\[\nZ_n(C)=I_n-O_n.\n\\]\nFinally,\n\\[\nJ_k=I_k+2O_k,\n\\]\nand even powers give nonnegativity.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "a uniform gap from two initial purities", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For every even \\(L\\ge4\\), let \\(H_L\\) be a non-scalar finite-dimensional self-adjoint Hamiltonian.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(a\\in\\mathbb R\\) and, for every \\(b&gt;0\\), a real finite or countable list \\((\\lambda_i(b))\\) satisfy\n  \\[\n  \\sum_i|\\lambda_i(b)|^4&lt;\\infty,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  Z_L(b):=\\operatorname{Tr}e^{-b(H_L+aLI)}\n  =\\sum_i|\\lambda_i(b)|^L\n  \\qquad(L\\ge4,\\ L\\text{ even}).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For even \\(n\\ge4\\), define\n  \\[\n  S(n,b)=\\frac{Z_{2n}(b)}{Z_n(b)^2},\n  \\qquad\n  T(n,b)=\\frac{Z_n(2b)}{Z_n(b)^2}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  f(u)=\\frac{u^2}{2(1-u)^2},\n  \\qquad\n  G(u)=\\frac12\n  \\left((1-u)^{-1}+(1-u)^{-2}+(1-u)^{-3}\\right)^2.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n_0\\ge4\\) be even and \\(b_0&gt;0\\). Suppose\n  \\[\n  0&lt;u_0&lt;\\frac16,\\qquad\n  G(u_0)\\le C,\\qquad\n  r:=Cu_0&lt;1,\\qquad\n  C(1-3u_0)&gt;3,\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  S(n_0,b_0)\\ge1-u_0,\n  \\qquad\n  T(2n_0,b_0)\\ge1-u_0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(j\\ge0\\), set\n  \\[\n  n_j=2^jn_0,\\qquad\n  b_j=2^jb_0,\\qquad\n  u_j=C^{-1}r^{2^j}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(j\\ge0\\),\n\\[\nS(n_j,b_j)\\ge1-u_j,\n\\qquad\nT(2n_j,b_j)\\ge1-u_j.\n\\]\nFor every even \\(L\\ge n_0\\), the physical ground state is unique, and the gap above it satisfies\n\\[\n\\gamma_L&gt;\\frac{\\log(1/r)}{b_0}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The function \\(G\\) is increasing on \\([0,1)\\), and\n\\[\nf\\!\\left(1-(1-u)^3\\right)=G(u)u^2.\n\\]</p><p>**Doubling.** The definition gives\n\\[\nu_{j+1}=Cu_j^2,\n\\qquad\n0&lt;u_{j+1}&lt;u_j\\le u_0.\n\\]\nSuppose both defects at \\((n_j,b_j)\\) are at most \\(u=u_j\\). The first bound in Lemma 3.2 is at most\n\\[\nf\\!\\left(1-(1-u)^3\\right)\n=G(u)u^2\n\\le Cu^2\n=u_{j+1}\n\\le u.\n\\]\nUse \\(p_+=u_{j+1}\\) in the second update. Its input is at most \\(1-(1-u)^3\\), so its output is also at most \\(u_{j+1}\\). Induction proves the two dyadic estimates.</p><p>**Intermediate even lengths.** For any nonnegative list \\((a_i)\\) with finite positive \\(p\\)-th power sum and \\(q\\ge p&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_i a_i^q\\le\\left(\\sum_i a_i^p\\right)^{q/p}.\n\\]\nIndeed, the normalized \\(p\\)-th powers are probabilities, whose \\(q/p\\)-th powers sum to at most one.</p><p>Apply this to the absolute spatial eigenvalues at each of the two temperatures. If \\(n\\le L\\le2n\\) and both lengths are even, then\n\\[\nZ_L(b)\\le Z_n(b)^{L/n},\n\\qquad\nZ_L(2b)\\ge Z_{2n}(2b)^{L/(2n)}.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nT(L,b)\n\\ge\n\\left[T(2n,b)S(n,b)^2\\right]^{L/(2n)}.\n\\]\nThe bracket lies in \\((0,1]\\), and the exponent lies in \\([1/2,1]\\). Taking \\(n=n_j\\), \\(b=b_j\\), gives\n\\[\nT(L,b_j)\\ge(1-u_j)^3\\ge1-3u_j&gt;\\frac12\n\\]\nfor every even \\(L\\in[n_j,2n_j]\\).</p><p>**The physical spectrum.** The largest physical Gibbs weight is at least the purity:\n\\[\n\\sum_kw_k^2\\le(\\max_kw_k)\\sum_kw_k=\\max_kw_k.\n\\]\nIt is therefore greater than \\(1/2\\). Since these weights count every physical eigenvector, two ground-state eigenvectors would give two equal maximal weights, which is impossible. The ground state is unique.</p><p>Since \\(H_L\\) is non-scalar, a next distinct energy exists. Let \\(w_0\\) be the ground-state weight and \\(w_1\\) the weight of an eigenvector at that next energy. Then\n\\[\ne^{-b_j\\gamma_L}\n=\\frac{w_1}{w_0}\n\\le\\frac{1-w_0}{w_0}\n\\le\\frac{3u_j}{1-3u_j}\n\\le\n\\frac{3}{C(1-3u_0)}r^{2^j}\n&lt;r^{2^j}.\n\\]\nThe shift \\(aL\\) cancels. Taking logarithms and using \\(b_j=2^jb_0\\) gives\n\\[\n\\gamma_L&gt;\\frac{\\log(1/r)}{b_0}.\n\\]\nThe dyadic intervals \\([n_j,2n_j]\\) cover all even \\(L\\ge n_0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "squaring probabilities", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\((w_i)\\) be a finite or countable family of nonnegative numbers with\n\\[\n\\sum_iw_i=1.\n\\]\nLet\n\\[\nP=\\sum_iw_i^2,\n\\qquad\nP'=\\sum_i\\left(\\frac{w_i^2}{P}\\right)^2.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The purity \\(P\\) lies in \\((0,1]\\), and\n\\[\n1-P'\\le f(1-P).\n\\]\nThe function \\(f\\) is increasing on \\([0,1)\\). Consequently, under the assumptions of Proposition 3,\n\\[\n0&lt;S(n,b),T(n,b)\\le1,\n\\]\n\\[\n1-S(2n,b)\\le f(1-S(n,b)),\n\\]\n\\[\n1-T(n,2b)\\le f(1-T(n,b)).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>At least one weight is positive, so \\(P&gt;0\\), and \\(w_i^2\\le w_i\\) gives \\(P\\le1\\). Nonnegativity permits rearrangement of all countable sums. Thus\n\\[\n1-P'\n=\n\\frac{2\\sum_{i&lt;j}w_i^2w_j^2}{P^2}\n\\le\n\\frac{2\\left(\\sum_{i&lt;j}w_iw_j\\right)^2}{P^2}\n=\n\\frac{(1-P)^2}{2P^2}.\n\\]\nAlso,\n\\[\nf'(u)=\\frac{u}{(1-u)^3}\\ge0.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(S(n,b)\\), use the spatial probabilities\n\\[\n\\frac{|\\lambda_i(b)|^n}{Z_n(b)}.\n\\]\nSquaring them changes \\(n\\) to \\(2n\\).</p><p>For \\(T(n,b)\\), list every physical eigenvalue \\(\\varepsilon_k(n)\\), with multiplicity, and use\n\\[\nw_k(n,b)\n=\n\\frac{e^{-b(\\varepsilon_k(n)+an)}}{Z_n(b)}.\n\\]\nTheir purity is \\(T(n,b)\\), and squaring them changes \\(b\\) to \\(2b\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "the coupled update", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the setting and purities of Proposition 3. Suppose \\(n\\ge4\\) is even, \\(b&gt;0\\), and \\(p,q\\in[0,1)\\) satisfy\n\\[\n1-S(n,b)\\le p,\\qquad\n1-T(2n,b)\\le q.\n\\]\nLet \\(p_+,q_+\\in[0,1)\\) obey\n\\[\np_+\\ge f\\!\\left(1-(1-p)^2(1-q)\\right),\n\\]\n\\[\nq_+\\ge f\\!\\left(1-(1-q)^2(1-p_+)\\right).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n1-S(2n,2b)\\le p_+,\n\\qquad\n1-T(4n,2b)\\le q_+.\n\\]\nThe first output may be rounded upward before it is used in the second update, and the second output may then be rounded upward.</p>", "proof": "<p>Direct cancellation gives\n\\[\nS(n,2b)\n=\nS(n,b)^2\\frac{T(2n,b)}{T(n,b)^2},\n\\]\n\\[\nT(4n,b)\n=\nT(2n,b)^2\\frac{S(2n,2b)}{S(2n,b)^2}.\n\\]\nBoth denominators are at most one.</p><p>The first identity gives\n\\[\nS(n,2b)\\ge(1-p)^2(1-q).\n\\]\nSpatial squaring in Lemma 3.1 and monotonicity of \\(f\\) prove the first assertion.</p><p>The second identity then gives\n\\[\nT(4n,b)\\ge(1-q)^2(1-p_+).\n\\]\nTemporal squaring proves the second assertion. Every input to \\(f\\) lies in \\([0,1)\\), because the product subtracted from one is positive and at most one.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "finite thermal inputs", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the physical twisted Hamiltonians and shifted partition functions of Proposition 1, with\n\\[\na=\\frac{700741}{500000}.\n\\]\nThe twists are \\(I,P,C\\), as in Proposition 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The following integers are centers for the shifted partition functions, in units \\(10^{-6}\\).</p><p>At \\(b=21/2\\), the absolute errors are less than \\(10^{-5}\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|r|r|r}\nn&amp;I&amp;P&amp;C\\\\ \\hline\n6&amp;8945303&amp;346734&amp;939987\\\\\n8&amp;3741281&amp;567167&amp;984108\\\\\n10&amp;2327547&amp;721090&amp;1002613\n\\end{array}\n\\]</p><p>At \\(b=49/4\\), the absolute errors are less than \\(30\\cdot10^{-6}\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|r|r}\nn&amp;I&amp;P\\\\ \\hline\n4&amp;124885379&amp;65757\\\\\n5&amp;2925&amp;2960742\\\\\n6&amp;12870614&amp;286599\\\\\n7&amp;54069&amp;1920414\\\\\n8&amp;4639283&amp;510140\\\\\n9&amp;195455&amp;1514846\\\\\n10&amp;2654823&amp;675419\\\\\n11&amp;377629&amp;1314544\\\\\n12&amp;1900547&amp;787405\n\\end{array}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 4.1 proves the \\(b=21/2\\) table, and Lemma 4.2 proves the \\(b=49/4\\) table. Both calculations use the physical twisted Hamiltonians and the same shift \\(a=700741/500000\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**The thermal calculation in original Appendix A.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**The thermal calculation in original Appendix A.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(b=21/2\\), \\(n\\in\\{6,8,10\\}\\), and \\(g\\in\\{I,P,C\\}\\). Let \\(c_{n,g}\\) be the corresponding first-table center in Proposition 4, multiplied by \\(10^{-6}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n|Z_n(21/2,g)-c_{n,g}|&lt;10^{-5}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>After the global rotations of Proposition 1, the twist angles are \\(\\theta=0,\\pi,2\\pi/3\\). Write \\(H=H_n(g)\\) and set\n\\[\nW=I-\\frac{H+(3n/2)I}{16},\n\\qquad\nB=16W.\n\\]\nProposition 1 gives \\(\\|W\\|\\le1\\) and \\(\\|B\\|\\le16\\).</p><p>In a fixed total-weight sector, rows and columns are words \\(x\\in\\{-1,0,1\\}^n\\), and\n\\[\nB_{x,x}=16-\\frac{3n}{2}-\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}x_{j-1}x_j.\n\\]\nFor each \\(j\\), put \\(k=j+1\\pmod n\\). If\n\\[\ny_j=x_j-\\delta,\\qquad y_k=x_k+\\delta,\n\\qquad \\delta\\in\\{-1,1\\},\n\\]\nand all letters remain in \\(\\{-1,0,1\\}\\), the corresponding entry is \\(-1\\), except at the seam \\(j=n-1,k=0\\), where it is \\(-e^{i\\theta\\delta}\\).</p><p>For the cyclic twist, computations are in \\(\\mathbb Z[\\omega]\\), \\(\\omega=e^{2\\pi i/3}\\), with\n\\[\n-\\omega(u+v\\omega)=v+(v-u)\\omega,\n\\]\n\\[\n-\\omega^{-1}(u+v\\omega)=(u-v)+u\\omega.\n\\]</p><p>Only representative columns are computed. For\n\\[\ns(x)=(x_{n-1},x_0,\\ldots,x_{n-2}),\n\\]\nthe seam formula gives\n\\[\nH_{s(x),s(y)}\n=e^{i\\theta(y_{n-1}-x_{n-1})}H_{x,y}.\n\\]\nThus the monomial unitary\n\\[\n|x\\rangle\\longmapsto e^{-i\\theta x_{n-1}}|s(x)\\rangle\n\\]\ncommutes with \\(H\\). Reversal of a word takes \\(H\\) to its complex conjugate, as does negation of every letter. Composing either permutation with complex conjugation gives an antiunitary symmetry. These symmetries preserve the norm of each corresponding column of a real exponential of \\(H\\).</p><p>In each nonnegative total-weight sector, choose the lexicographically least word in every rotation-and-reversal orbit. Its multiplicity is the orbit size, doubled for strictly positive weight to include the opposite sector. The multiplicities sum to \\(3^n\\) and reproduce the full Frobenius norm of the exact exponential. No symmetry of rounded columns is required.</p><p>Put\n\\[\nE=e^{84(W-I)}\n=e^{-(b/2)(H+(3n/2)I)},\n\\qquad\nx_n=(3/2-a)bn.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nZ_n(b,g)=e^{x_n}\\|E\\|_F^2.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(0\\le j\\le256\\), define\n\\[\np_j=\\frac{84^j/j!}{\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{256}84^\\ell/\\ell!},\n\\qquad\nP(W)=\\sum_{j=0}^{256}p_jW^j.\n\\]\nThese are Poisson probabilities of mean \\(84\\), conditioned on values at most \\(256\\). For a Poisson variable \\(V\\) of mean \\(84\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr(V&gt;256)\n\\le\\frac{\\mathbb E(2^V)}{2^{257}}\n=\\frac{e^{84}}{2^{257}}\n&lt;2^{-117}.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\|W^j\\|\\le1\\), conditioning changes the operator average by at most twice this probability. Hence\n\\[\n\\|P(W)-E\\|&lt;2^{-116}.\n\\]</p><p>Set\n\\[\nQ=2^{56},\\qquad q_j=\\lfloor Qp_j\\rfloor.\n\\]\nFor each representative column \\(r\\), start at zero and perform\n\\[\nz\\longleftarrow \\operatorname{fl}(Bz/16)+q_je_r\n\\qquad(j=256,\\ldots,0).\n\\]\nFor real coordinates, \\(\\operatorname{fl}\\) is the ordinary floor; for \\(u+v\\omega\\), it floors both integer coordinates separately.</p><p>The ideal suffix at step \\(j\\) is\n\\[\nQ\\sum_{\\ell=j}^{256}p_\\ell W^{\\ell-j}e_r,\n\\]\nwhose norm is at most \\(Q\\). In a sector of dimension \\(d_0\\), one coordinatewise floor has Euclidean error less than \\(2\\sqrt{d_0}\\), and one coefficient floor adds at most one. Contraction gives the accumulated bound\n\\[\n257(2\\sqrt{3^n}+1)\\le125159.\n\\]\nThe zero coefficients above \\(q_{172}\\) may be omitted without changing the computed vector; the bound still counts all \\(257\\) possible steps.</p><p>The norm of the repeated computed-column list, divided by \\(Q\\) and multiplied by \\(e^{x_n/2}\\), differs from \\(\\sqrt{Z_n(b,g)}\\) by at most\n\\[\ne^{x_n/2}\\sqrt{3^n}\n\\left(\n\\frac{257(2\\sqrt{3^n}+1)}{Q}+2^{-116}\n\\right)\n&lt;4\\cdot10^{-7}.\n\\]\nHere \\(\\sqrt{3^n}\\le243\\), \\(x_n/2&lt;6\\), and \\(e^{x_n/2}&lt;3^6\\), giving the stated rational bound.</p><p>The calculation packs batches of at most \\(192\\) columns:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{pack}(y)\n=\\sum_{t=0}^{w-1}y_t2^{64t}.\n\\]\nInteger linear arithmetic preserves this identity before flooring, even if intermediate carries occur. For a cyclic coordinate,\n\\[\n|u|,|v|\n\\le\\frac2{\\sqrt3}|u+v\\omega|\n\\le2|u+v\\omega|.\n\\]\nThus every coordinate of \\(Bz\\), in either integer component, has magnitude at most\n\\[\n32(Q+125159)\n=2305843009217699040\n&lt;2^{63}.\n\\]</p><p>Let\n\\[\ns_w=\\sum_{t=0}^{w-1}2^{64t},\n\\quad\n\\mathrm{bias}=2^{63}s_w,\n\\quad\n\\mathrm{mask}=(2^{60}-1)s_w,\n\\quad\n\\mathrm{bias16}=2^{59}s_w.\n\\]\nFor \\(Y=\\operatorname{pack}(y)\\) satisfying the preceding bound, the operation\n\\[\n(((Y+\\mathrm{bias})\\mathbin{\\gg}4)\\mathbin{\\&amp;}\\mathrm{mask})\n-\\mathrm{bias16}\n\\]\nequals\n\\[\n\\operatorname{pack}\\bigl((\\lfloor y_t/16\\rfloor)_{t=0}^{w-1}\\bigr).\n\\]\nAdding the bias makes each base-\\(2^{64}\\) digit lie in \\([0,2^{64})\\). The global shift moves four low bits from each higher lane into the four high bits of the preceding lane; the mask deletes exactly those crossing bits. Subtracting \\(2^{59}\\) gives the floor, including for negative digits. Hence the packed recurrence is exactly the integer recurrence.</p><p>Final coordinates satisfy \\(2(Q+125159)&lt;2^{63}\\), so biasing and unpacking recover them exactly. A coordinate \\(u+v\\omega\\) has squared modulus\n\\[\nu^2-uv+v^2.\n\\]\nSumming with representative multiplicities gives the following exact squared norms \\(t_{n,g}\\) in \\(Q\\)-units:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|r}\nn&amp;g&amp;t_{n,g}\\\\ \\hline\n6&amp;I&amp;93637037510293158533967385845216\\\\\n6&amp;P&amp;3629519998654831111446492683663\\\\\n6&amp;C&amp;9839536903588146388838689926305\\\\\n8&amp;I&amp;4947327904487991279560670127283\\\\\n8&amp;P&amp;749999795795292705866802820621\\\\\n8&amp;C&amp;1301346729026243689610427021457\\\\\n10&amp;I&amp;388818111664728958208623998490\\\\\n10&amp;P&amp;120458568770462253326886628639\\\\\n10&amp;C&amp;167487042402286114495695779656\n\\end{array}\n\\]</p><p>For the scalar exponential, put\n\\[\nL_n=\\sum_{j=0}^{100}\\frac{x_n^j}{j!},\n\\qquad\nU_n=L_n+\\frac{2x_n^{101}}{101!}.\n\\]\nSince \\(0&lt;x_n&lt;12\\), successive terms after the first omitted term have ratio at most \\(x_n/102&lt;1/2\\). Thus\n\\[\nL_n&lt;e^{x_n}&lt;U_n.\n\\]\nExact rational comparison gives\n\\[\nc_{n,g}-2\\cdot10^{-6}\n&lt;\n\\frac{L_nt_{n,g}}{Q^2}\n&lt;\n\\frac{U_nt_{n,g}}{Q^2}\n&lt;\nc_{n,g}+2\\cdot10^{-6}.\n\\]\nIn particular, every computed scaled norm is below \\(4\\). Replacing its square by the true trace therefore incurs error less than\n\\[\n(8+4\\cdot10^{-7})\\,4\\cdot10^{-7}.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n|Z_n(21/2,g)-c_{n,g}|\n&lt;\n2\\cdot10^{-6}\n+(8+4\\cdot10^{-7})\\,4\\cdot10^{-7}\n&lt;10^{-5}.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**The thermal calculation in original Appendix B.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**The thermal calculation in original Appendix B.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let\n\\[\nb=\\frac{49}{4},\\qquad 4\\le n\\le12,\\qquad g\\in\\{I,P\\}.\n\\]\nLet \\(C_{n,g}\\) be the corresponding integer center in the second table of Proposition 4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\left|Z_n(49/4,g)-\\frac{C_{n,g}}{10^6}\\right|\n&lt;30\\cdot10^{-6}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Work in weight blocks. The twist \\(P\\) is represented by a rotation through \\(\\pi\\) about the weight-basis axis. Set\n\\[\nJ=280,\\qquad Q=2^{55},\n\\]\n\\[\nR=I-\\frac{(3n/2)I+H}{16},\n\\qquad\nD=32R.\n\\]\nThe matrix \\(D\\) is integral. At a word \\(w\\),\n\\[\nD_{w,w}=32-3n-2\\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}w_{i-1}w_i.\n\\]\nAn allowed opposite-unit step at sites \\(i-1,i\\) has entry \\(-2\\), except that it has entry \\(+2\\) at the twisted seam \\(i=0\\). The unit-step coefficient follows from the two ladder factors \\(\\sqrt2\\) and the transverse prefactor \\(1/2\\).</p><p>Proposition 1 gives\n\\[\n0\\le\\frac{3n}{2}I+H\\le\\frac{5n}{2}I,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\n\\operatorname{spec}(R)\n\\subset[1-5n/32,1]\\subset[-7/8,1].\n\\]\nThus \\(R\\) is a self-adjoint contraction.</p><p>Put\n\\[\nF=\\exp\\!\\left[-\\frac b2\\left(\\frac{3n}{2}I+H\\right)\\right]\n=\\exp[98(R-I)],\n\\]\n\\[\nx_n=bn(3/2-a).\n\\]\nFor direct sums over all weight blocks,\n\\[\nZ_n(b,g)=e^{x_n}\\|F\\|_F^2.\n\\]</p><p>Define\n\\[\nv_j=98^j\\frac{J!}{j!},\n\\qquad\np_j=\\frac{v_j}{\\sum_{\\ell=0}^Jv_\\ell}\n\\qquad(0\\le j\\le J).\n\\]\nThese are conditioned Poisson weights of mean \\(98\\). Since \\(R\\) is a contraction,\n\\[\n\\left\\|F-\\sum_{j=0}^Jp_jR^j\\right\\|\n\\le\n2e^{-98}\\sum_{j=281}^\\infty\\frac{98^j}{j!}\n\\le2^{-280}e^{98}\n&lt;2^{-84}\n&lt;Q^{-1}.\n\\]\nFor the second inequality use \\(2^j\\ge2^{281}\\) in the tail and\n\\[\ne^{-98}\\sum_{j\\ge0}\\frac{196^j}{j!}=e^{98};\n\\]\nthe next uses \\(e&lt;4\\). The estimate includes the normalization change.</p><p>For each selected basis column \\(e_r\\), start with \\(X=0\\) and perform\n\\[\nX\\longleftarrow\n\\left\\lfloor\\frac{DX}{32}\\right\\rfloor+c_je_r,\n\\qquad\nc_j=\\lfloor Qp_j\\rfloor,\n\\qquad\nj=J,J-1,\\ldots,0.\n\\]\nThe vector floor is coordinatewise. The coefficients vanish for \\(j&gt;191\\), so those initial zero steps may be omitted.</p><p>Compare with the ideal suffix\n\\[\nY_j=Q\\sum_{\\ell=j}^Jp_\\ell R^{\\ell-j}e_r,\n\\qquad\n\\|Y_j\\|_2\\le Q.\n\\]\nIn a block of dimension \\(d_0\\), one vector floor gives error less than \\(\\sqrt{d_0}\\), and one coefficient floor gives error less than one. Set\n\\[\nd_n=\\lfloor\\sqrt{3^n}\\rfloor+1,\n\\qquad\nE_n=281(d_n+1)+1,\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal E_n=d_nE_n.\n\\]\nIncluding the exponential-approximation error, each computed column satisfies\n\\[\n\\|X-QFe_r\\|_2\\le E_n.\n\\]\nAll \\(281\\) ideal steps are charged, including the omitted steps whose integer coefficients vanish.</p><p>The recurrence can be evaluated exactly in signed \\(64\\)-bit integers. Every intermediate coordinate has absolute value at most\n\\[\nQ+281(d_n+1)&lt;2Q,\n\\qquad d_n\\le730.\n\\]\nThe absolute sum of a row of \\(D\\) is at most\n\\[\n|32-3n|+2n+4n\\le76.\n\\]\nThe first two terms bound the diagonal, and at most two hops per bond give the last term. Thus every product and partial sparse-multiplication sum has absolute value less than\n\\[\n76\\cdot2Q\n=5476377146882523136\n&lt;2^{63}-1.\n\\]\nDivision and coefficient addition also fit, since\n\\[\n\\frac{76\\cdot2Q}{32}+Q\n=207165582859042816\n&lt;2^{63}-1.\n\\]\nStarting from zero, these bounds first establish exactness of the next operation; contraction then establishes the next coordinate bound. Final squares and sums use arbitrary-precision integers.</p><p>For positive total weight, use rotation-and-reversal word orbits and give each representative twice its orbit size. In the zero-weight block include digit negation in the orbit and use its size without doubling. Choose the lexicographically least representative. The multiplicities \\(\\nu_r\\) satisfy\n\\[\n\\sum_r\\nu_r=3^n.\n\\]</p><p>Reversal fixes the seam as an unordered bond, and global negation preserves every Hamiltonian entry. For the twisted chain, left rotation by \\(q\\) moves the seam to \\((k-1,k)\\), \\(k=-q\\pmod n\\). The diagonal unitary\n\\[\nG_k|w\\rangle\n=(-1)^{\\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}w_j}|w\\rangle,\n\\qquad G_0=I,\n\\]\nflips hopping signs exactly at the two boundary bonds of the arc, restoring the original seam. The resulting signed shift commutes with \\(H\\). Therefore exact exponential columns at orbit-related words have equal norms.</p><p>Let \\(X_r\\) be the computed representative column and set\n\\[\nW_{n,g}=\\sum_r\\nu_r\\sum_wX_r(w)^2.\n\\]\nCompare abstract direct sums repeating each exact and computed representative \\(\\nu_r\\) times. Their squared norms are \\(Q^2\\|F\\|_F^2\\) and \\(W_{n,g}\\). Consequently,\n\\[\n\\left|\\sqrt{W_{n,g}}-Q\\|F\\|_F\\right|\n\\le\\sqrt{3^n}E_n\n&lt;\\mathcal E_n.\n\\]\nNo equivariance of rounded columns is required.</p><p>The integer recurrence gives\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|r|r}\nn&amp;W_{n,I}&amp;W_{n,P}\\\\ \\hline\n4&amp;1298092848946206529211955779616040&amp;683496640248050713756440676071\\\\\n5&amp;9095379218684502088743241789&amp;9205948219074287936685289758037\\\\\n6&amp;11971284263768914176431179720622&amp;266573596119930013833919176645\\\\\n7&amp;15044150708248976451784593468&amp;534330762492192297990602166370\\\\\n8&amp;386135473475360677201628357188&amp;42459883501386986922766507327\\\\\n9&amp;4866417737994347551816846903&amp;37716485750353424806876104310\\\\\n10&amp;19772960374215472827775892486&amp;5030484777222468978572235564\\\\\n11&amp;841347687722481981930750335&amp;2928767918985354021900689432\\\\\n12&amp;1266666171463993322695232916&amp;524785463625387865619045258\n\\end{array}\n\\]</p><p>For rational enclosures, put\n\\[\nr_{n,g}=\\lfloor\\sqrt{W_{n,g}}\\rfloor,\n\\qquad\ns_n=\\sum_{j=0}^{100}\\frac{x_n^j}{j!},\n\\qquad\nt_n=\\frac{x_n^{100}}{100!}.\n\\]\nSince \\(0\\le x_n&lt;15\\), the omitted tail is at most \\(15t_n/86&lt;t_n\\). Hence\n\\[\ns_n\\le e^{x_n}\\le s_n+t_n.\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\nL_{n,g}\n=\n\\frac{s_n\\max(0,r_{n,g}-\\mathcal E_n)^2}{Q^2},\n\\]\n\\[\nU_{n,g}\n=\n\\frac{(s_n+t_n)(r_{n,g}+1+\\mathcal E_n)^2}{Q^2}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nL_{n,g}\\le Z_n(b,g)\\le U_{n,g}.\n\\]\nAll endpoints are rational or integer; the integer square root is determined by\n\\[\nr_{n,g}^2\\le W_{n,g}&lt;(r_{n,g}+1)^2.\n\\]</p><p>Direct integer and rational evaluation yields\n\\[\nL_{n,g}&gt;\\frac{C_{n,g}-30}{10^6},\n\\qquad\nU_{n,g}&lt;\\frac{C_{n,g}+30}{10^6}\n\\]\nfor all eighteen cases. Every strict inequality has margin greater than \\(13/10^6\\). These enclosures prove the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "the two variational inputs", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(H_N\\) be the untwisted periodic spin-one Heisenberg Hamiltonian, and set\n\\[\na=\\frac{700741}{500000}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nE_0(72)&lt;-72a,\n\\qquad\nE_0(120)&lt;-120a.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\nZ_{72}(b)&gt;1,\\qquad Z_{120}(b)&gt;1\n\\qquad(b&gt;0).\n\\]\nThese are variational bounds for the minimum of the full physical spectrum.</p>", "proof": "<p>For both \\(N=72\\) and \\(N=120\\), Lemma 5.2 gives\n\\[\n\\frac{U_N}{V_N}\n&lt;\n\\frac{-c_N+1}{10^{12}}\n&lt;\n-\\frac{700741}{500000}\n=-a.\n\\]\nApply Lemma 5.1 to obtain\n\\[\nE_0(N)&lt;-Na\n\\]\nand \\(Z_N(b)&gt;1\\) for every \\(b&gt;0\\).</p><p>The support rule puts these trial vectors in total weight zero, but the variational bound concerns the full physical spectrum. The only symmetry used in the contraction proof is cyclic site translation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "norm and energy contractions", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(N\\ge4\\), \\(D\\ge1\\), and let \\(A_{-1},A_0,A_1\\in M_D(\\mathbb R)\\). Define\n\\[\n\\psi_N(s_0,\\ldots,s_{N-1})\n=\\operatorname{Tr}(A_{s_0}\\cdots A_{s_{N-1}})\n\\]\nin the standard orthonormal spin-one weight basis. Put\n\\[\nB_{st}=A_sA_t,\n\\qquad\nT=\\sum_{s=-1}^1A_s\\otimes A_s,\n\\]\n\\[\nO=\n\\sum_{s,t=-1}^1st\\,B_{st}\\otimes B_{st}\n+\n\\sum_{\\substack{s=-1,0\\\\t=0,1}}\n\\left(\nB_{st}\\otimes B_{s+1,t-1}\n+\nB_{s+1,t-1}\\otimes B_{st}\n\\right).\n\\]\nAll matrix traces are ordinary unnormalized traces. Set\n\\[\nV_N=\\operatorname{Tr}(T^N),\n\\qquad\nU_N=\\operatorname{Tr}(T^{N-2}O).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\|\\psi_N\\|^2=V_N,\n\\qquad\n\\langle\\psi_N,H_N\\psi_N\\rangle=NU_N.\n\\]\nIf \\(V_N&gt;0\\) and \\(U_N&lt;-aV_N\\), then\n\\[\nE_0(N)&lt;-Na,\n\\qquad\nZ_N(b)&gt;1\\quad(b&gt;0).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Expand \\(T^N\\) and use\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}(X\\otimes Y)\n=\\operatorname{Tr}(X)\\operatorname{Tr}(Y).\n\\]\nSince all amplitudes of \\(\\psi_N\\) are real, this is the sum of their squares. No self-adjointness or positivity of the finite contraction matrix \\(T\\) is assumed.</p><p>In the weight basis, a bond has diagonal entry \\(st\\). Its off-diagonal entries are one between\n\\[\n(s,t)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad(s+1,t-1),\n\\qquad s=-1,0,\\quad t=0,1.\n\\]\nThis follows from\n\\[\nh=S^z\\otimes S^z\n+\\frac12(S^+\\otimes S^-+S^-\\otimes S^+)\n\\]\nand ladder entries \\(\\sqrt2\\). Inserting the bond into the two amplitude layers gives exactly \\(O\\). Summing the other physical indices therefore gives \\(U_N\\) for one bond.</p><p>Cyclicity of the amplitude trace makes \\(\\psi_N\\) invariant under cyclic site translation, so every bond, including the periodic seam, contributes equally.</p><p>For \\(V_N&gt;0\\), the variational principle yields\n\\[\nE_0(N)\\le\\frac{NU_N}{V_N}&lt;-Na.\n\\]\nThe shifted ground energy is negative, so its term in \\(Z_N(b)\\) exceeds one.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**The explicit integer matrix triple and contractions of original Appendix C.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**The explicit integer matrix triple and contractions of original Appendix C.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Set \\(D=26\\). Index virtual types by \\(\\alpha=1,\\ldots,8\\), with\n\\[\n(\\ell_1,\\ldots,\\ell_8)=(1,1,1,1,3,3,3,5).\n\\]\nOrder virtual labels \\((\\alpha,d)\\) first by \\(\\alpha\\), then by\n\\[\nd=-\\ell_\\alpha,-\\ell_\\alpha+2,\\ldots,\\ell_\\alpha.\n\\]</p><p>Use\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|rrrrrrrr}\n\\alpha&amp;1&amp;2&amp;3&amp;4&amp;5&amp;6&amp;7&amp;8\\\\ \\hline\nD_\\alpha&amp;-289&amp;-84&amp;-9&amp;33&amp;-22&amp;27&amp;71&amp;-11\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nand the following parameters, defined when \\(\\ell_\\beta=\\ell_\\alpha+2\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|r@{\\qquad}c|r@{\\qquad}c|r}\n(\\alpha,\\beta)&amp;P_{\\alpha\\beta}\n&amp;(\\alpha,\\beta)&amp;P_{\\alpha\\beta}\n&amp;(\\alpha,\\beta)&amp;P_{\\alpha\\beta}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,5)&amp;-11&amp;(2,7)&amp;-49&amp;(4,6)&amp;9\\\\\n(1,6)&amp;-35&amp;(3,5)&amp;-6&amp;(4,7)&amp;-32\\\\\n(1,7)&amp;-28&amp;(3,6)&amp;-25&amp;(5,8)&amp;1\\\\\n(2,5)&amp;-23&amp;(3,7)&amp;-20&amp;(6,8)&amp;2\\\\\n(2,6)&amp;16&amp;(4,5)&amp;-34&amp;(7,8)&amp;-16\n\\end{array}\n\\]</p><p>For row \\((\\alpha,d)\\), column \\((\\beta,e)\\), and \\(s\\in\\{-1,0,1\\}\\), put \\(\\ell=\\ell_\\alpha\\), \\(k=\\ell_\\beta\\). The entry of \\(A_s\\) is zero unless\n\\[\ne=d+2s,\\qquad |k-\\ell|\\le2,\\qquad\n(k=\\ell\\Longrightarrow\\alpha=\\beta).\n\\]\nFor an allowed entry, define\n\\[\np=\n\\begin{cases}\nD_\\alpha,&amp;k=\\ell,\\\\\nP_{\\alpha\\beta},&amp;k=\\ell+2,\\\\\n-P_{\\beta\\alpha},&amp;k=\\ell-2,\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\nr=\\frac{d+\\ell}{2}.\n\\]\nThe entry is \\(pf\\), where\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|ccc}\n&amp;s=-1&amp;s=0&amp;s=1\\\\ \\hline\nk=\\ell+2&amp;\n2(\\ell+1-r)(\\ell+2-r)&amp;\n2(r+1)(\\ell-r+1)&amp;\n(r+1)(r+2)\\\\\nk=\\ell&amp;\n2(\\ell-r+1)&amp;d&amp;-(r+1)\\\\\nk=\\ell-2&amp;2&amp;-2&amp;1\n\\end{array}\n\\]\ndefines \\(f\\).</p><p>Form \\(T,O,U_N,V_N\\) as in Lemma 5.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(N=72\\) and \\(N=120\\),\n\\[\nV_N&gt;0,\n\\qquad\n-c_NV_N\\le10^{12}U_N&lt;(-c_N+1)V_N,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nc_{72}=1401482112601,\n\\qquad\nc_{120}=1401482105174.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The support rule makes both \\(T\\) and \\(O\\) preserve \\(d-e\\) on paired virtual labels \\(((\\alpha,d),(\\beta,e))\\). For \\(T\\), the two layers shift by the same \\(2s\\). For \\(O\\), the two layers have the same total physical weight \\(s+t\\), including each off-diagonal term.</p><p>The exhaustive difference blocks have dimensions\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|rrrrrrrrrrr}\nd-e&amp;-10&amp;-8&amp;-6&amp;-4&amp;-2&amp;0&amp;2&amp;4&amp;6&amp;8&amp;10\\\\ \\hline\n\\text{dimension}&amp;1&amp;8&amp;32&amp;80&amp;136&amp;162&amp;136&amp;80&amp;32&amp;8&amp;1\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nand sum to \\(676=26^2\\). Each block contributes once.</p><p>Form \\(A_s,B_{st},T,O\\) from the entry rules, and restrict to blocks \\(T_\\delta,O_\\delta\\). Compute powers by\n\\[\nP_0=I,\\qquad\nP_{2j}=P_j^2\\quad(j\\ge1),\n\\qquad\nP_{2j+1}=P_j^2T_\\delta\\quad(j\\ge0).\n\\]\nThen accumulate\n\\[\nU_N=\\sum_\\delta\\sum_{i,j}(P_{N-2})_{ij}(O_\\delta)_{ji},\n\\]\n\\[\nV_N=\\sum_\\delta\\sum_{i,j}(P_{N-2})_{ij}(T_\\delta^2)_{ji}.\n\\]\nOnly arbitrary-precision integer operations are used.</p><p>For \\(N=72\\), the required power is \\(70\\). For \\(N=120\\), one may use\n\\[\nT^{118}=T^{64}T^{32}T^{16}T^4T^2,\n\\qquad\nT^{120}=T^{64}T^{32}T^{16}T^8\n\\]\nwithin each block.</p><p>The resulting exact integer comparisons are precisely\n\\[\nV_N&gt;0,\\qquad\n-c_NV_N\\le10^{12}U_N&lt;(-c_N+1)V_N\n\\]\nwith the two stated values of \\(c_N\\).</p><p>The paper records the full contractions and the positive margins\n\\[\n-700741V_N-500000U_N\n\\]\nin\n\\[\n\\texttt{verification/computations/evidence/trials/certificate72.json}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\texttt{verification/computations/evidence/trials/certificate120.json}.\n\\]\nThe common matrices are recorded in\n\\[\n\\texttt{verification/computations/evidence/trials/matrix-data.json}.\n\\]\nThe program\n\\[\n\\texttt{verification/computations/trials/run\\_trial.py}\n\\]\nwith \\(\\texttt{--length 72}\\) or \\(\\texttt{--length 120}\\) executes the entry rules and block contractions in\n\\[\n\\texttt{verification/computations/trials/original\\_trial.py}.\n\\]\nThe displayed comparisons also follow directly from the recorded full integers.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "capped masses and polynomial filters", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions for the capped-mass assertion.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions for the capped-mass assertion.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\((t_i)\\) be a finite or countable family satisfying\n  \\[\n  0\\le t_i\\le h,\\qquad \\sum_it_i\\le m,\n  \\qquad h&gt;0,\\quad m\\ge0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(r\\ge1\\), define\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal C(m,h,r)=qh^r+(m-qh)^r,\n  \\qquad\n  q=\\lfloor m/h\\rfloor.\n  \\]</p><p>Capped-mass statement, original Lemma 5.2.\n\\[\n\\sum_it_i^r\\le\\mathcal C(m,h,r).\n\\]\nWithout the individual cap,\n\\[\n\\sum_it_i^r\\le m^r.\n\\]</p><p>Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions for the filter assertion.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\epsilon\\ge0\\), \\(v&gt;0\\), and let \\((\\lambda_i)\\) be a real list with absolutely convergent moments\n  \\[\n  M_j=\\sum_i\\lambda_i^j\n  \\]\n  at every index occurring in\n  \\[\n  F(x)=\\sum_jf_jx^j.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose\n  \\[\n  |M_j-c_j|\\le\\epsilon\n  \\]\n  at those indices and \\(F\\ge0\\) on \\(\\mathbb R\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  B=\\sum_jf_jc_j+\\epsilon\\sum_j|f_j|.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(F(x)&gt;B\\) whenever \\(|x|\\ge v\\).</p><p>Filter statement, original Lemma 5.3.\nEvery eigenvalue in the list satisfies\n\\[\n|\\lambda_i|&lt;v.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For a finite family at fixed total mass, transfer mass from the smaller of two coordinates in \\((0,h)\\) to the larger until one reaches \\(0\\) or \\(h\\). Convexity shows that the sum of \\(r\\)-th powers does not decrease. Repetition leaves at most one coordinate in \\((0,h)\\), giving the capped expression at the actual total mass.</p><p>That expression is continuous and nondecreasing in the total mass, so it is bounded by its value at \\(m\\). Apply this argument to finite subfamilies and take their increasing union for a countable family.</p><p>Without the cap, if the total mass is positive,\n\\[\nt_i^r\\le t_i\\left(\\sum_jt_j\\right)^{r-1}.\n\\]\nSumming proves the uncapped assertion; zero total mass is immediate.</p><p>For the filter, absolute convergence gives\n\\[\n\\sum_iF(\\lambda_i)\\le B.\n\\]\nEach summand is nonnegative. A single \\(|\\lambda_i|\\ge v\\) would therefore contradict \\(F(\\lambda_i)&gt;B\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "initialization at length sixty", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the shifted partition functions and spatial transfer representation of Propositions 1–2, with\n\\[\na=\\frac{700741}{500000}.\n\\]\nFor even \\(n\\ge4\\), define\n\\[\nS(n,b)=\\frac{Z_{2n}(b)}{Z_n(b)^2},\n\\qquad\nT(n,b)=\\frac{Z_n(2b)}{Z_n(b)^2}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At inverse temperature \\(b=105/4\\),\n\\[\nS(60,b)&gt;\\frac78,\n\\qquad\nT(120,b)&gt;\\frac78.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Begin at\n\\[\nb_0=\\frac{21}{2}.\n\\]\nFrom the corresponding table in Proposition 4 and the sector formulas in Proposition 2, upper bounds for \\(I_{10},O_{10},N_{10}\\) are\n\\[\nm_I=\\frac{1042655}{10^6},\n\\qquad\nm_O=\\frac{40041}{10^6},\n\\qquad\nm_N=\\frac{401625}{10^6}.\n\\]\nTheir margins over the sector centers plus \\(10^{-5}\\) are respectively\n\\[\n\\frac{19}{12\\cdot10^6},\n\\qquad\n\\frac7{12\\cdot10^6},\n\\qquad\n\\frac3{4\\cdot10^6}.\n\\]\nThe coefficient sums in the sector formulas have absolute sum at most one, so the thermal error also bounds each sector-moment error.</p><p>Apply the polynomial filter of Proposition 6 to\n\\[\nF_I(x)=x^6(-2+10x^2)^2,\n\\qquad\nF_N(x)=x^6(-25+100x^2)^2.\n\\]\nFor a filter \\(x^6(c_0+c_1x^2)^2\\), its summed upper bound is the centered combination of moments \\(6,8,10\\), with coefficients \\(c_0^2,2c_0c_1,c_1^2\\), plus\n\\[\n10^{-5}(|c_0|+|c_1|)^2.\n\\]\nDirect rational evaluation gives\n\\[\n|\\lambda|&lt;U:=\\frac{100245}{100000}\n\\quad\\text{in }I,\n\\]\n\\[\n|\\lambda|&lt;V:=\\frac{88643}{100000}\n\\quad\\text{in }N.\n\\]\nAt the indicated endpoints, filter values exceed their summed upper bounds by more than \\(0.0301\\) and \\(0.3463\\), respectively. Beyond each endpoint the filter is strictly increasing with \\(|x|\\), since \\(c_1&gt;0\\) and \\(c_0+c_1x^2&gt;0\\).</p><p>The rational inequalities\n\\[\nU^{10}&lt;m_I&lt;2U^{10},\n\\qquad\nV^{10}&lt;m_N&lt;2V^{10}\n\\]\nallow the two-piece capped-mass bound. For \\(k=30,120\\), put\n\\[\nA_k=U^k+(m_I-U^{10})^{k/10},\n\\]\n\\[\nB_k=m_O^{k/10},\n\\qquad\nC_k=V^k+(m_N-V^{10})^{k/10}.\n\\]\nThey bound \\(I_k,O_k,N_k\\), respectively. The moments are nonnegative at these even orders, so\n\\[\nZ_k(b_0)\\le A_k+2B_k+3C_k,\n\\]\n\\[\nZ_k(b_0,P),\\ Z_k(b_0,C)\\le A_k+2B_k.\n\\]</p><p>Set\n\\[\nm=A_{30}+2B_{30}+3C_{30},\n\\quad\np=A_{30}+2B_{30},\n\\quad\nq=A_{120}+2B_{120}+3C_{120},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nD=\\frac{1450}{1000},\n\\quad\nE=\\frac{1202}{1000},\n\\quad\n\\tau=\\frac{1225}{1000},\n\\quad\n\\tau_0=\\frac{1264}{1000},\n\\quad\nR_q=\\frac{684}{10000}.\n\\]\nThe required rational comparisons are\n\\[\nm^5&lt;D^2,\\qquad\np^5&lt;E^2,\\qquad\nq\\ge1,\\qquad\n(q-1)^5&lt;R_q^2,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{D+11E}{12}\\le\\tau,\n\\qquad\n\\frac{D+3E}{4}\\le\\tau_0.\n\\]\nThe positive margins in the first, second, and fourth strict inequalities exceed \\(0.001068\\), \\(0.000433\\), and \\(0.0000472\\), respectively.</p><p>Now increase the inverse temperature to\n\\[\nb=\\frac52b_0=\\frac{105}{4}.\n\\]\nFor positive Boltzmann factors \\(z_i\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_i z_i^{5/2}\\le\\left(\\sum_i z_i\\right)^{5/2}.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\nZ_{30}(b)\\le D,\n\\qquad\nZ_{30}(b,P),Z_{30}(b,C)\\le E.\n\\]\nThe \\(I\\) and \\(J\\) portions of the thirtieth spatial moment are therefore at most \\(\\tau\\) and \\(\\tau_0\\).</p><p>Let\n\\[\nw_i=|\\lambda_i(X_b)|^{30}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\sum_iw_i\\le D,\n\\qquad\n\\sum_iw_i^4=Z_{120}(b)&gt;1\n\\]\nby Proposition 5. Since\n\\[\nD(0.88)^3&lt;1,\n\\]\nsome weight \\(w_0&gt;0.88\\) exists. It is unique because \\(2(0.88)&gt;D\\). It belongs to \\(I\\), since a weight in \\(O\\) occurs at least twice and a weight in \\(N\\) at least three times. This counts all multiplicities, including equal moduli of opposite-sign spatial eigenvalues.</p><p>To sharpen the lower bound on \\(w_0\\), suppose \\(0\\le t\\le0.998\\) and \\(w_0\\ge t\\). Write \\(x\\) for the remaining \\(I\\)-mass, \\(y\\) for the full \\(O\\)-pair mass, and \\(z\\) for the full \\(N\\)-triple mass. Their squared weights sum to at most\n\\[\nx^2+\\frac{y^2}{2}+\\frac{z^2}{3},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n0\\le x\\le\\tau-t,\\qquad\n0\\le y,\\qquad\nx+y\\le\\tau_0-t,\n\\]\n\\[\nz\\le D-t-x-y.\n\\]\nThe divisors \\(2,3\\) use the identical repeated-sector lists. Since \\(D&gt;\\tau_0\\), the last upper bound is nonnegative on the entire polygon.</p><p>The convex quadratic\n\\[\nx^2+\\frac{y^2}{2}+\\frac{(D-t-x-y)^2}{3}\n\\]\nhas its maximum at one of the four vertices\n\\[\n\\mathcal V_t=\n\\{(0,0),(\\tau-t,0),(\\tau-t,\\tau_0-\\tau),(0,\\tau_0-t)\\}.\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\nR(t)=\n\\max_{(x,y)\\in\\mathcal V_t}\n\\left(\nx^2+\\frac{y^2}{2}+\\frac{(D-t-x-y)^2}{3}\n\\right).\n\\]\nThen \\(R(t)\\) bounds the remaining squared weights, and \\(R(t)^2\\) bounds their fourth powers.</p><p>Exact evaluation gives\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|ccc}\nt&amp;0.88&amp;0.99&amp;0.998\\\\ \\hline\nR(t)&amp;0.1359&amp;0.0721&amp;0.068404\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n1-0.99^4-R(0.88)^2=0.02093518,\n\\]\n\\[\n1-0.998^4-R(0.99)^2=0.002777621984.\n\\]\nIf \\(w_0\\le0.99\\), the first equality contradicts \\(\\sum_iw_i^4&gt;1\\). The second then excludes \\(w_0\\le0.998\\).</p><p>Keeping the dominant contribution in the fourth moment,\n\\[\nS(60,b)\n=\n\\frac{\\sum_iw_i^4}{(\\sum_iw_i^2)^2}\n\\ge\n\\left(1+\\frac{R(0.998)}{0.998^2}\\right)^{-2}\n&gt;0.8756&gt;\\frac78.\n\\]</p><p>For the physical purity at length \\(120\\), return to \\(b_0\\). Its largest physical Boltzmann factor \\(z_0\\) exceeds one by Proposition 5, while all factors sum to at most \\(q\\). After raising the factors to the power \\(5/2\\), the ratio of remaining mass to \\(z_0^{5/2}\\) is at most\n\\[\n(q-1)^{5/2}&lt;R_q.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nT(120,b)\\ge(1+R_q)^{-2}&gt;0.8760&gt;\\frac78.\n\\]\nThis last step concerns physical Boltzmann factors separately from the spatial weights.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "initialization giving the stronger gap, including its intermediate periodic data", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the shifted partition functions, spatial operator, and sectors of Propositions 1–2, with\n\\[\na=\\frac{700741}{500000},\n\\]\nand define \\(S,T\\) as in Proposition 7. Put\n\\[\nb_0=\\frac{49}{4},\n\\qquad\nv_J=\\frac{100139}{100000},\n\\qquad\nv_N=\\frac{872}{1000},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\ell=\\frac{999}{1000},\n\\qquad\nm_J=1.0657205,\n\\qquad\nm_N=0.2783155.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At \\(b=b_0\\), every eigenvalue in \\(J\\) has modulus less than \\(v_J\\), every eigenvalue in each \\(N\\) list has modulus less than \\(v_N\\), and\n\\[\nJ_{12}\\le m_J,\\qquad N_{12}\\le m_N.\n\\]\nThere is an eigenvalue in \\(J\\) whose modulus exceeds \\(\\ell\\).</p><p>At the first doubled pair,\n\\[\n1-S(72,49/2)\\le\\frac{151249}{2500000}.\n\\]\nAfter six coupled updates, at \\(b=784\\),\n\\[\n1-S(2304,b)\\le\\frac{84513}{10^7}&lt;\\frac1{100},\n\\]\n\\[\n1-T(4608,b)\\le\\frac{27493}{5\\cdot10^6}&lt;\\frac1{100}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>At \\(b_0=49/4\\), Proposition 4 and the sector formulas in Proposition 2 give centers for \\(J_n,N_n\\), with error at most\n\\[\n\\epsilon=30\\cdot10^{-6}.\n\\]\nThe sector coefficient sums have absolute sum at most one, so this error also applies to each sector moment. Odd moments retain their signs.</p><p>Use the filters\n\\[\nF_J(x)\n=x^4(7+17x-280x^2-185x^3+1000x^4)^2,\n\\]\n\\[\nF_N(x)\n=x^4(12-394x^2+1000x^4)^2.\n\\]\nTheir degree is twelve, so the table supplies every needed moment. The summed upper bounds in Proposition 6 are exactly\n\\[\nB_J=318604.54848175,\n\\qquad\nB_N=48169.880699.\n\\]\nDirect rational comparisons give\n\\[\nF_J(v_J)-B_J&gt;180,\n\\]\n\\[\nF_J(-v_J)-B_J&gt;496888,\n\\]\n\\[\nF_N(\\pm v_N)-B_N&gt;654.\n\\]</p><p>Write\n\\[\nQ(x)=7+17x-280x^2-185x^3+1000x^4.\n\\]\nFor \\(y\\ge0\\),\n\\[\nQ(1+y)\n=559+2902y+5165y^2+3815y^3+1000y^4,\n\\]\n\\[\nQ(-1-y)\n=895+3978y+6275y^2+4185y^3+1000y^4.\n\\]\nBoth are positive and increasing. The polynomial\n\\[\n12-394r^2+1000r^4\n\\]\nis positive and increasing for \\(r\\ge v_N\\): it is positive at \\(v_N\\), and its derivative is\n\\[\n4r(1000r^2-197)&gt;0.\n\\]\nThus the filters increase along the required exterior rays. Proposition 6 gives the stated modulus bounds.</p><p>The twelfth-moment upper bounds from the table are\n\\[\nm_J=1.0657205,\\qquad m_N=0.2783155.\n\\]\nFor \\(k=36,72\\), define\n\\[\nB(k)=3\\mathcal C(m_N,v_N^{12},k/12),\n\\]\n\\[\nR_A(k)=B(k)+(m_J-\\ell^{12})^{k/12}.\n\\]\nHere \\(m_J&gt;\\ell^{12}\\).</p><p>If every \\(J\\)-eigenvalue had modulus at most \\(\\ell\\), Proposition 6 would imply\n\\[\nZ_{72}(b_0)\n\\le\n\\mathcal C(m_J,\\ell^{12},6)+B(72)\n&lt;1.\n\\]\nThe strict margin to one exceeds \\(0.0693\\). This contradicts Proposition 5. Choose a \\(J\\)-eigenvalue \\(\\lambda_0\\) with\n\\[\n|\\lambda_0|&gt;\\ell.\n\\]</p><p>After removing it, the remaining twelfth-moment mass in \\(J\\) is at most \\(m_J-\\ell^{12}\\). The uncapped bound of Proposition 6, together with the three \\(N\\) sectors, shows that \\(R_A(k)\\) bounds the remaining full \\(k\\)-th moment.</p><p>Consequently,\n\\[\n1-S(36,b_0)\\le p_0\n:=\n1-\\left(1+\\frac{R_A(36)}{\\ell^{36}}\\right)^{-2},\n\\]\n\\[\n1-T(72,b_0)\\le q_0\n:=\n1-\\left(v_J^{72}+R_A(72)\\right)^{-2}.\n\\]\nFor the first estimate, retain \\(\\lambda_0^{72}\\) in the numerator. For the second use\n\\[\nZ_{72}(2b_0)&gt;1,\n\\qquad\nZ_{72}(b_0)\\le v_J^{72}+R_A(72).\n\\]\nNumerically,\n\\[\np_0&lt;0.047916,\\qquad q_0&lt;0.181521,\n\\]\nbut the updates use the exact rational definitions.</p><p>Apply the coupled update of Proposition 3, as established by its Lemma 3.2, six times. Round each new defect upward to a multiple of \\(10^{-7}\\), using the rounded spatial defect in the physical update. With\n\\[\n\\lceil x\\rceil_7=10^{-7}\\lceil10^7x\\rceil,\n\\]\nthe updates are\n\\[\np_{j+1}\n=\n\\left\\lceil\nf\\!\\left(1-(1-p_j)^2(1-q_j)\\right)\n\\right\\rceil_7,\n\\]\n\\[\nq_{j+1}\n=\n\\left\\lceil\nf\\!\\left(1-(1-q_j)^2(1-p_{j+1})\\right)\n\\right\\rceil_7.\n\\]\nExact rational evaluation gives\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\nj&amp;p_j&amp;q_j\\\\ \\hline\n1&amp;151249/2500000&amp;433453/2500000\\\\\n2&amp;343303/5000000&amp;1632381/10000000\\\\\n3&amp;44601/625000&amp;361773/2500000\\\\\n4&amp;632939/10000000&amp;1055161/10000000\\\\\n5&amp;375793/10000000&amp;445941/10000000\\\\\n6&amp;84513/10000000&amp;27493/5000000\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nEvery update lies in \\([0,1)\\), where \\(f\\) is increasing, so upward rounding preserves the estimates.</p><p>The first row gives the asserted bound at \\((72,49/2)\\). After six steps, length and inverse temperature have both multiplied by \\(2^6\\):\n\\[\n36\\cdot64=2304,\n\\qquad\n\\frac{49}{4}\\cdot64=784.\n\\]\nThe last row proves the final two bounds.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 2, "cite"], [3, 0, "cite"], [3, 6, "cite"], [3, 13, "cite"], [3, 14, "cite"], [3, 15, "cite"], [3, 20, "cite"], [3, 21, "cite"], [4, 3, "cite"], [5, 3, "cite"], [6, 0, "cite"], [6, 1, "cite"], [6, 13, "cite"], [6, 20, "cite"], [6, 21, "cite"], [7, 6, "cite"], [7, 8, "cite"], [7, 9, "cite"], [8, 6, "cite"], [9, 6, "cite"], [10, 0, "cite"], [10, 21, "cite"], [11, 12, "cite"], [12, 10, "cite"], [12, 21, "cite"], [13, 20, "cite"], [13, 21, "cite"], [14, 13, "cite"], [15, 13, "cite"], [16, 1, "cite"], [16, 20, "cite"], [16, 21, "cite"], [17, 16, "cite"], [17, 18, "cite"], [18, 16, "cite"], [18, 19, "cite"], [19, 20, "cite"], [19, 21, "cite"], [20, 0, "cite"], [20, 21, "cite"], [21, 0, "cite"], [21, 1, "cite"]]});
